#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edc9e8c-08d5-5dcf-a96b-da7718b22b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098179} {:answer/id #uuid "92156ab8-1052-580a-83a7-d00eb9b7fbe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098180} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fed2fe-fbf7-5ab6-94ca-6e278e7405ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098181} {:answer/id #uuid "c836692a-82a4-5def-8e9e-9be589689e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098182} {:answer/id #uuid "1e07085c-ba56-572a-be0d-35f636270823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e07085c-ba56-572a-be0d-35f636270823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098183} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf814557-78d5-5344-93ec-163339cb31eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b2e480d-7f17-59ba-a02c-a786007bf39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140631" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{5}.$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b393b878-c5bd-525b-95d3-a6f539ad09f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098191} {:answer/id #uuid "cc57abfc-d7d4-5bbe-b820-47d6bcfddc83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098192} {:answer/id #uuid "f102d687-4edb-5d57-a6d8-6e06a4e9175f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098193} {:answer/id #uuid "01ed849a-83c5-5a2e-9479-ecd16db6de49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098194} {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c7473c-7e8a-5122-8097-f7924bdb001a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b66471-b70f-5509-8368-6a6674b74447" :question/math-academy-id "q-141078" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0f8d466-522b-5e38-8b40-10106f232e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310497} {:answer/id #uuid "d43b4b10-8063-5466-be3a-a3c3af6b66cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310498} {:answer/id #uuid "e002db91-eb89-5d8f-867f-e2b8d1fe3b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310499} {:answer/id #uuid "30059b5a-4016-5fc7-8da6-22330210c414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310500} {:answer/id #uuid "e092af6a-ef57-5a2a-bda8-9a05223e717d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30059b5a-4016-5fc7-8da6-22330210c414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310500} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b7284a-24a3-59f0-acf0-ff9f993c64d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c5d8fd2-fb66-5109-b8f5-6406e18d1fe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141080" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f544ad4c-287f-5f45-bf2b-4ef526b966c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310504} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8f82be-8027-57f2-bd5a-4060f0575094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310505} {:answer/id #uuid "f4965f74-f800-520e-9e40-e0efcb2f4993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310506} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1aaf8e-926d-59d2-8b66-4e1ff4215aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310507} {:answer/id #uuid "eda4b349-f3ac-5703-b224-8300d536a6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f544ad4c-287f-5f45-bf2b-4ef526b966c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310504} :answer-field/id #uuid "140c983c-b009-5108-b1e0-38503d18bbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90a72911-f2a4-5e18-9c98-33431b3777b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-141082" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd0170d-f326-58f5-9749-6c0c86a31a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310511} {:answer/id #uuid "786090ba-9cbf-54ea-a394-9daafec6aad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310512} {:answer/id #uuid "b123aa26-72ff-5beb-b46d-3ca35327d703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310513} {:answer/id #uuid "91713830-f892-5573-a648-0a2929a227e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310514} {:answer/id #uuid "f65988d8-7b63-5a3f-868e-57f1ee94e5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91713830-f892-5573-a648-0a2929a227e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310514} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd61664-dc80-5ee5-b6d5-ed8564b05673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca0e4ee1-6292-559e-9935-18a9adc4fe22" :question/math-academy-id "q-141086" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f7892e-2376-5f4d-ab2f-2fc9850117bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e99f4-4cc4-5dd5-b7e6-5456542cb904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415} {:answer/id #uuid "91627b8a-627f-5c2e-a6ff-0ff3d52ce635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416} {:answer/id #uuid "24d20f25-8bb9-5064-94e6-5bca9c6df55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417} {:answer/id #uuid "1aea66b2-c4cb-5cf9-b060-bc56cab96f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aea66b2-c4cb-5cf9-b060-bc56cab96f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ce3d7f-290b-5336-8578-d6aa19601a4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78b7d940-8463-5444-b751-5735936b4729" :question/math-academy-id "q-141093" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8eb959c-4147-5c9f-87ec-004b09f3501f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281428} {:answer/id #uuid "22c8ebdf-8e74-5323-9672-5c39508def7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281429} {:answer/id #uuid "27b529fd-6543-558f-9aeb-7d399d8bf7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281430} {:answer/id #uuid "625d9350-740f-5374-b637-d8e7c15778af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281431} {:answer/id #uuid "582b77a2-82b2-521f-aa21-1eea403a5828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb959c-4147-5c9f-87ec-004b09f3501f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281428} :answer-field/id #uuid "75912ee8-4ef3-5ab6-ad3b-7e543bef68c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06bd8f05-4440-5830-a789-d3d0eb85823a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eda923f-1829-5e8c-88f7-3943b21e23fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281435} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ba2b9-eed2-57d8-8720-a71caa9e2f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436} {:answer/id #uuid "47d9e7d0-433c-50c0-b8b8-873dc870e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281437} {:answer/id #uuid "edadaffd-dff1-5ac4-bcbd-3ab12dd79405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438} {:answer/id #uuid "80ce65d4-a0f0-5dcf-a74a-2943f8ee9d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d9e7d0-433c-50c0-b8b8-873dc870e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281437} :answer-field/id #uuid "d70d9c7f-1be6-57d6-9f0a-ad943b684745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "482773e5-5b8a-5acd-8d88-a970f8a254f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141103" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ce4f98-69c1-5501-9df2-126de889fad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310518} {:answer/id #uuid "d496640d-5dc3-53d1-8063-49bf44149943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310519} {:answer/id #uuid "da38b541-6ec6-5af7-b366-35bb89be2a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310520} {:answer/id #uuid "223e5c53-9d6a-57a8-acdb-78b763a3f1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310521} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd20074-d614-5913-957c-3e6b685ffdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d496640d-5dc3-53d1-8063-49bf44149943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310519} :answer-field/id #uuid "021c376f-b348-53b0-90c8-42e126191cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53021b4a-ecf6-51c5-9c2a-a4f9c53c3409" :question/math-academy-id "q-141108" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-x} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{-0} \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=1{e}^{-x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5c774d-afee-57d7-84cf-a0112e0f4265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310525} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5bf16a-f239-598e-9644-b30e5b4ee7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310526} {:answer/id #uuid "df57b554-74a6-5f0c-9daa-e52643a8a0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310527} {:answer/id #uuid "acdc6c56-2315-55fb-9644-96753e024035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310528} {:answer/id #uuid "6a024b94-cda4-5d3b-941c-aaf7e25ae8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee5c774d-afee-57d7-84cf-a0112e0f4265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310525} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d367f92-bd39-543c-a262-08c67e072c59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d075088-7ad8-5baf-a890-9be7b4897d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-141109" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb3568c7-c17b-5e67-a3aa-8dea513766cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310532} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bb263f-9077-5675-9736-f53a031858a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310533} {:answer/id #uuid "52a93f1f-33aa-5383-b49b-499a8be03dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310534} {:answer/id #uuid "c57409c9-1851-52d1-b5f9-bee377268cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310535} {:answer/id #uuid "9789d49a-4682-5fae-b2ce-a88ed84ce36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a93f1f-33aa-5383-b49b-499a8be03dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310534} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb6aab2-0868-5dad-a0ff-dabac8d0133b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4a5499b-2fc3-56dd-b2e1-0663916bd75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141111" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf997eb-6058-5c08-a5f0-d2d144aba426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9f9779-ffa0-5156-bb77-9064e661447e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cf997eb-6058-5c08-a5f0-d2d144aba426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455} :answer-field/id #uuid "07c72c27-a9d0-5f4d-9333-493f99092f6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53d754a0-a4b9-5c1a-ac3a-5eea26512dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352655" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc7ac89-5c15-5d25-b143-d16c7a705d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f53f988-495a-5c39-b3eb-0c8f7fdbc467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc7ac89-5c15-5d25-b143-d16c7a705d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310540} :answer-field/id #uuid "114bbd7e-95c4-5d24-8516-9822b36b1568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "550459fd-d04d-5c33-8efe-cdcd1b67f25f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352656" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y(-1)=4.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4e & =A \\\\ A & =4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2982eb2-2159-59db-9ba1-3b0538fe51a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283974} {:answer/id #uuid "34c1ba00-0e56-5150-9cab-44a2b3259f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283975} {:answer/id #uuid "84d06fc0-4ed4-560c-8333-af3559a8f046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283976} {:answer/id #uuid "1ceb600f-0104-52b3-ab17-027a337a6cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283977} {:answer/id #uuid "8969a8b6-7e3b-5c4d-b05f-c8c253f7596e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2982eb2-2159-59db-9ba1-3b0538fe51a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db0b1ef-c8dc-580f-8d9d-3d36b51250fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "026a0cae-bdc3-5eb5-888c-83ba0c1115ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-79333" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\frac{7}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=\\frac{2}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5098d2-8759-5c88-873b-59c47317c676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100303} {:answer/id #uuid "bef0901a-3e08-5d64-8008-69c96233a746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100304} {:answer/id #uuid "ef60a1c2-3509-51f2-bca5-8747b265f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100305} {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} {:answer/id #uuid "14f11516-8f03-5153-b333-a0fbd914b758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} :answer-field/id #uuid "917fb1fb-b6b6-5fde-815a-f160d6a1eef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a773fc2b-812d-5f5c-8cbb-9b1672c48928" :question/math-academy-id "q-79335" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
